        {
          "turn": 6,
          "prompt": "Explain how src/inventory/reorder.py decides what to order.",
          "answer": "`src/inventory/reorder.py` implements a **two-part reorder policy**:\n\n---\n\n### 1. `reorder_due()` \u2014 Which SKUs to check\n- **Logic**: Returns every SKU whose current level is at or below `REORDER_THRESHOLD` (12)\n- **Implementation**: Iterates over all items via `all_items()`, calls `level_for(sku)` for each, and filters where `level <= REORDER_THRESHOLD`\n- **Result**: A list of SKUs that need attention\n\n### 2. `order_size(sku)` \u2014 How much to order\n- **Formula**: `shortfall = REORDER_THRESHOLD * 3 - level_for(sku)`\n  - The goal is to bring stock up to **3\u00d7 the threshold** (i.e., 36 units)\n  - `shortfall` = target (36) minus current level\n- **Cap**: `min(max(shortfall, 0), MAX_BATCH)`\n  - `max(shortfall, 0)` ensures you never order a negative amount (if stock is already above 36, result is 0)\n  - `MAX_BATCH` (250) caps the maximum order quantity\n- **Example values**:\n  - If SKU has **0** stock \u2192 order `min(36, 250)` = **36**\n  - If SKU has **10** stock \u2192 order `min(26, 250)` = **26**\n  - If SKU has **40+** stock \u2192 order **0** (no reorder needed)\n\n### Summary of the policy\n| Condition | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Current level \u2264 12 (REORDER_THRESHOLD) | SKU appears in `reorder_due()` list |\n| Order calculated as `36 - current_level`, capped at 250 (MAX_BATCH) | Quantity to request from supplier |\n| Current level > 36 | No order placed (shortfall \u2264 0) |\n\nThe system thus maintains a \"target inventory\" of 36 units per SKU, but respects a maximum batch limit of 250 units per order.",
