          "turn": 7,
          "prompt": "What does src/shipping/carriers.py do?",
          "answer": "`src/shipping/carriers.py` \u2014 **Carrier selection with automatic failover**. It manages three shipping providers and their pricing, plus a failure-tracking system that skips carriers after repeated rejections.\n\n---\n\n### Carrier definitions\n| Carrier | Base fee | Per-kg rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ravenpost` | $4.20 | $0.85 |\n| `tortoise` | $2.10 | $1.60 |\n| `kestrel` | $6.00 | $0.40 |\n\n### Failure tracking\n- `_failures` starts at `{name: 0 for name in CARRIERS}` \u2014 counts rejections per carrier\n- **3 consecutive failures** \u2192 the carrier is marked \"dead\" for the rest of that run\n- The next-cheapest available carrier takes over automatically\n\n### Key functions\n\n| Function | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `quote(name, weight_kg)` | Returns the shipping cost: `round(base + per_kg \u00d7 weight_kg, 2)` |\n| `cheapest(weight_kg)` | Filters out carriers with \u22653 failures, then returns the cheapest remaining one via `min(..., key=quote)` |\n| `record_failure(name)` | Increments that carrier's failure count and returns the new total |\n\n### How it works in practice\n1. An order comes in with a weight\n2. `cheapest(weight_kg)` is called to get the lowest-cost carrier that's still \"live\" (failures < 3)\n3. If a label is rejected, `record_failure(carrier_name)` is called\n4. After 3 failures, that carrier is excluded from future `cheapest()` calls for the rest of the run\n5. The next cheapest available carrier automatically takes over \u2014 this is silent by design (per the runbook)\n\n### Pricing crossover points\n- **tortoise vs kestrel**: tortoise becomes cheaper at ~1.75 kg (`2.10 + 1.60w < 6.00 + 0.40w \u2192 w > 1.75`)\n- **ravenpost vs tortoise**: ravenpost becomes cheaper at ~3.75 kg (`4.20 + 0.85w < 2.10 + 1.60w \u2192 w > 3.75`)",
