          "answer": "Together, these two tiny utility modules provide **pure string and time helpers** with no external dependencies:\n\n---\n\n### `src/util/text.py` \u2014 Text manipulation (16 lines)\n\n| Function | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `slugify(value)` | Lowercases the input, replaces all non-alphanumeric characters with hyphens via regex `[^a-z0-9]+`, then strips leading/trailing hyphens. E.g., `\"Hello World!\"` \u2192 `\"hello-world\"`. |\n| `truncate(value, limit=40)` | If `len(value) > limit`, returns the first `limit - 1` characters followed by an ellipsis `\u2026`. Otherwise returns the value unchanged. The default cap is 40 chars. |\n\n---\n\n### `src/util/time.py` \u2014 Time arithmetic (13 lines)\n\n| Function / Constant | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `SECONDS_PER_DAY = 86400` | Constant for converting between seconds and days. |\n| `days_between(earlier, later)` | Returns the whole number of days between two epoch timestamps: `int((later - earlier) // SECONDS_PER_DAY)`. |\n| `is_stale(timestamp, now, ttl)` | Returns `True` once `timestamp` is older than `ttl` seconds: `(now - timestamp) >= ttl`. No timezone handling \u2014 works purely with raw epoch seconds. |\n\n---\n\n### Common pattern\nBoth modules export **small, focused, pure functions** that operate on primitive types (strings, integers) and are easy to test in isolation. They're the kind of glue code you'd typically find in a utility package \u2014 no classes, no state, no side effects.",
